    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> had been reconquered for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a> in 535 and was a peripheral Byzantine province when Muslim forces from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a> began raiding it in 652. Syracuse was taken in 740, but a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> revolt forced the invaders home. In 826 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphemius">Euphemius</a>, commander of the Byzantine fleet of Sicily, rebelled after Emperor Michael II ordered his nose cut off for forcing a nun to marry him. Driven out, he offered the island to Ziyadat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a>, the Aghlabid emir of Ifriqiya.</p><p>The conquest was entrusted to the 70-year-old qadi Asad ibn al-Furat, with 10,000 infantry, 700 cavalry and 100 ships. They landed at Mazara del Vallo and won the first battle on 15 July 827. Plague killed Asad during the siege of Syracuse, and Euphemius died at Castrogiovanni. In 830 30,000 Ifriqiyan and Andalusi troops arrived, and in September 831 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> fell after a year-long siege. It became the Muslim capital, renamed al-Madinah, "The City". The conquest was a see-saw affair: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messina">Messina</a> fell in 842 or 843, Syracuse in 878 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taormina">Taormina</a> in 902, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rometta">Rometta</a> held out until 965.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The new Arab rulers carried out land reforms that ended the dominance of the large estates and encouraged smallholdings. They improved irrigation with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qanat">qanats</a> and introduced lucrative crops, including oranges, lemons, pistachio and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugarcane">sugarcane</a>, as well as vegetables, dates and mulberry trees where only wheat had been grown for centuries, and mining began. The coins were the gold dinar of 4.25 grams and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> dirhem of 2.97 grams.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> was a major cultural and political centre of the Muslim world. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-hawqal">Ibn Hawqal</a>, who visited in 950, described the walled Kasr with its great Friday Mosque and the suburb of al-Khalisa (Kalsa) with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a>'s palace, baths, government offices and a prison. Its population is uncertain: Paul Bairoch estimated 350,000 in the 11th century, Stephan R. Epstein closer to 60,000, Kenneth Meyer Setton between 100,000 and 250,000. The island became multiconfessional and multilingual, an Arab-Byzantine culture of Arab and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> migrants and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, Greek and Jewish communities. Christians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were tolerated as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhimmi">dhimmis</a> but paid the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizya">jizya</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharaj">kharaj</a>; about half the population was Muslim by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norman-conquest">Norman Conquest</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The conquest itself took most of the 9th century, against Byzantine troops backed at Syracuse by a Venetian fleet under Doge Giustiniano Participazio. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messina">Messina</a> became a base for campaigns into the Italian mainland. Under the conquest's rules the fighters, the junud, took four-fifths of conquered land as booty and one fifth went to the state, the khums.</p><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalbid-dynasty">Kalbids</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taormina">Taormina</a> was conquered again in 962 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rometta">Rometta</a> soon after. Raids into Southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> went on into the 11th century, and in 982 a Christian army under the Emperor Otto II and the brothers Landulf and Pandulf was defeated at Stilo near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crotone">Crotone</a>. In 1038 a Byzantine army under George Maniaces, with a corps of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varangians">Varangians</a> including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-hardrada">Harald Hardrada</a>, crossed the strait of Messina and took Syracuse, but a Muslim counter-offensive won back all the cities lost. The Normans under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-i-of-sicily">Roger de Hauteville</a> then invaded. A small Norman force defeated the Sicilians and North Africans at the Battle of Cerami in 1063, and in 1068 Roger defeated the Zirid Ayyub at the Battle of Misilmeri. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> fell on 10 January 1072 after a five-month siege.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The emir, seated in what is now the Royal Palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a>, held authority over the army, administration, justice and the treasury. He appointed the governors of major cities, the high judges (qadi) and arbitrators for minor disputes (hakam). An assembly of notables, the giamà'a, advised him and sometimes made decisions in his place. The authorities very likely ran a tiraz, an official workshop making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and other fine fabrics as gifts.</p><p>The island was divided into three vals: Val di Mazara in the west, more Islamized and directly administered; Val Demone in the northeast, majority Christian and often in revolt, where government focused on taxes and order; and Val di Noto in the southeast. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aghlabid-dynasty">Aghlabids</a> ruled until 909, when the Shiite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> replaced them. Sicilian Muslims, mostly Sunni, resisted the new rulers: the first Fatimid governor was expelled in 912, Ibn Qurhub rebelled until 916, and a revolt of 937 was suppressed only in 941. In 948 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> al-Mansur appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasan-al-kalbi">al-Hasan ibn Ali al-Kalbi</a>, and after the Fatimids moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 972–973 they recognized the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalbid-dynasty">Kalbids</a> as hereditary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> became a prosperous and influential commercial power in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, and the wealth of its new crops and irrigation led to a flourishing of art and science. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a>, beautified with gardens and public works, was compared with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>; the traveller Ibn Jubair, writing at the end of the 12th century, praised its splendour, its four springs and mosques too many to count, most of them also schools. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> refugees from the North African rebellions of 943–947 against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> settled on the island.</p><p>Arabic remained a language of government for at least a century under Norman rule, and Muslim craftsmanship, masonry and administrative skill were sought after. The Norman kingdom under Roger II has been called multi-ethnic and religiously tolerant. Traces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> remain in modern Sicily: minor Arabic influence in the Sicilian language and in place names, and a much larger one in Maltese, which derives from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siculo-arabic">Siculo-Arabic</a>. Other remnants are found in the island's farming methods and crops, its cuisine and its architecture.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> Yusuf al-Kalbi a period of steady decline began. Under Ahmad ibn Yusuf al-Akhal dynastic conflict grew, with factions of the ruling family allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> or the Zirids, and al-Mu'izz ibn Badis tried and failed to annex the island. In 1044, under al-Hasan al-Samsam, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> broke into four qadits: those of Abdallah ibn Mankut, Ibn al-Hawwas, Ibn al-Maklati and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-al-thumna">Ibn al-Thumna</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> came under a council of sheikhs. Ibn al-Thumna worked closely with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> against his rivals, and after his death in 1062 Muslim troops made up the majority of the Hauteville army in Sicily.</p><p>A Zirid army under Ali and Ayyub ibn Tamim was beaten at Cerami and Misilmeri and went home. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catania">Catania</a> fell in 1071 and Palermo in 1072. Ibn Abbad of Syracuse held out until he died in 1086; Hammud of Castrogiovanni surrendered and converted in 1087, and in 1091 Butera, Noto and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a> fell. Muslims long remained a majority and served at the royal court, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> pogroms began in the 1160s. Frederick II deported about 60,000 Muslims to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucera">Lucera</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a> from 1223, many of them enslaved, and the removal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> was complete by the late 1240s.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> was the island's Islamic state, built by the Aghlabid conquest from 827 and ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a>, from 948 by the Kalbid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> under Fatimid authority. Its new crops and irrigation made it rich and its culture mixed. It broke into rival qadits in the 1040s and fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> by 1091.</em></p>

